{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.12.12","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[{"sourceType":"competition","sourceId":129872,"databundleVersionId":15939853}],"dockerImageVersionId":31286,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# CircleID ICDAR 2026 - Pen Classification\n---\n\n**What is this competition?**\n\nWe are given thousands of scanned PNG images of hand-drawn circles. Each circle was drawn by a different person using one of 8 different pens. Our job is to look at a circle image and predict which pen (numbered 1 to 8) was used to draw it.\n\nThink of it like a handwriting expert who can tell just by looking at the ink whether someone used a ballpoint pen, a fountain pen, or a felt-tip marker except here a neural network learns to do that automatically.\n\nThe test set contains circles drawn by writers the model has never seen before. So the model cannot just memorise which writer uses which pen. It must learn the actual visual texture and thickness of each pen's ink stroke.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 1 - Setup and Imports\n\nBefore writing any machine learning code we need to install and import the right tools. Think of this section like unpacking your toolbox before starting a project.\n\n**What each library does in plain English:**\n\n- **torch / torchvision** - The core deep learning engine. It does the maths of training neural networks, runs them on the GPU, and handles tensors (the multi-dimensional arrays that store image data).\n- **timm** - A library full of pretrained neural networks ready to use. Instead of training from scratch (which takes weeks), we download a network already trained on millions of photos and fine-tune it on our circle images.\n- **albumentations** - A fast image augmentation library. Augmentation means randomly modifying training images (flipping, rotating, adjusting brightness) so the model sees more variety and doesn't memorise the training set.\n- **pandas / numpy** - Standard tools for loading CSV files and doing array maths.\n- **sklearn** — Provides the stratified train/validation split and evaluation metrics.\n- **matplotlib / seaborn** - For plotting charts and visualising results.\n- **PIL** - The Python Imaging Library, used to open and inspect image files.\n- **tqdm** - Prints a live progress bar while the model trains.\n\n**Why do we fix random seeds?**\n\nNeural networks start with random weights, and data loading involves random shuffling. If we set the same seed (42) everywhere before running the code, those random choices are always the same. This means the experiment is reproducible: running the notebook twice gives the same result.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Install required packages\nimport subprocess, sys\n\ndef pip_install(*packages):\n    subprocess.check_call(\n        [sys.executable, '-m', 'pip', 'install', '-q', *packages]\n    )\n\npip_install('timm>=0.9.16', 'albumentations>=1.3.1', 'tqdm')\nprint('Libraries installed.')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:15:31.679028Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:15:31.679373Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:15:35.906272Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:15:31.679344Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:15:35.905306Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Standard library\nimport os, random, warnings\nfrom pathlib import Path\nwarnings.filterwarnings('ignore')\n\n# Data handling\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Visualisation\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\nfrom PIL import Image\n\n# Machine learning utilities\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom sklearn.metrics import f1_score, confusion_matrix, ConfusionMatrixDisplay\n\n# Deep learning core\nimport torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.optim as optim\nfrom torch.utils.data import Dataset, DataLoader\nfrom torch.cuda.amp import GradScaler, autocast\nimport timm\n\n# Augmentation library\nimport albumentations as A\nfrom albumentations.pytorch import ToTensorV2\n\n# Progress bars\nfrom tqdm.auto import tqdm\n\nprint(f'PyTorch      : {torch.__version__}')\nprint(f'timm         : {timm.__version__}')\nprint(f'Albumentations: {A.__version__}')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:15:35.908318Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:15:35.908743Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:15:35.916857Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:15:35.908715Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:15:35.915980Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Global configuration** - I define every important number in one place. This is good practice: if you want to try 30 epochs instead of 20, you change one line here rather than hunting through the whole notebook.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# All tunable settings live here \nSEED         = 42        # Fixed seed for reproducibility\nIMG_SIZE     = 224       # Every image is resized to 224 x 224 pixels\nBATCH_SIZE   = 32        # 32 images processed together per training step\nNUM_EPOCHS   = 20        # Number of full passes through the training data\nLR           = 3e-4      # Starting learning rate\nWEIGHT_DECAY = 1e-2      # Regularisation penalty on large weights\nNUM_CLASSES  = 8         # Eight pens = eight classes\nLABEL_SMOOTH = 0.1       # Prevents the model from being overconfident\nMODEL_NAME   = 'efficientnetv2_s'  # Pretrained backbone from timm\nNUM_WORKERS  = 2         # Background CPU processes that pre-load images\nTTA_STEPS    = 5         # Number of augmented views used at test time\n\n# Where the competition data lives \n# On Kaggle the path is /kaggle/input/<dataset-name>.\n# If you run locally, point DATA_DIR to your own folder.\nDATA_DIR = Path('/kaggle/input/competitions/icdar-2026-circleid-pen-classification')\nif not DATA_DIR.exists():\n    DATA_DIR = Path('.')   # Fallback: current working directory\n    print(f'[INFO] DATA_DIR set to: {DATA_DIR.resolve()}')\n\nCHECKPOINT  = Path('best_model.pth')    # Where to save the best model\nSUBMIT_PATH = Path('submission_pen.csv') # Final submission file\n\n# Detect GPU \n# If a GPU is available we use it. Otherwise training falls back to CPU,\n# which is much slower but still works for testing the pipeline.\nDEVICE = torch.device('cuda' if torch.cuda.is_available() else 'cpu')\nprint(f'Device: {DEVICE}')\nif DEVICE.type == 'cuda':\n    print(f'GPU   : {torch.cuda.get_device_name(0)}')\n\n# Fix all random seeds\n# We lock the seed in Python, NumPy, and PyTorch so that every random\n# choice (weight initialisation, batch shuffling, augmentations) is the\n# same every time this notebook is run from top to bottom.\ndef seed_everything(seed: int = SEED):\n    random.seed(seed)\n    os.environ['PYTHONHASHSEED'] = str(seed)\n    np.random.seed(seed)\n    torch.manual_seed(seed)\n    torch.cuda.manual_seed_all(seed)\n    torch.backends.cudnn.deterministic = True\n    torch.backends.cudnn.benchmark     = False\n\nseed_everything(SEED)\nprint(f'Seed {SEED} fixed across Python / NumPy / PyTorch.')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:15:35.918025Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:15:35.918295Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:15:35.959007Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:15:35.918270Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:15:35.957591Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 2 - Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)\n\nBefore building any model it is essential to look at the data. EDA stands for Exploratory Data Analysis — it just means 'take a careful look before you start'. A few minutes of EDA prevents hours of debugging later.\n\n**What I want to find out:**\n\n1. Are the eight pen classes evenly represented, or is one pen rare? If class 3 has 10x more images than class 7, the model will be biased toward predicting class 3 and we need to account for that.\n2. What do the actual circle images look like? Are the scans clean or noisy? Do circles from different pens look visually different?\n3. Are all images the same size? Most neural networks need a fixed input size, so knowing the range of image dimensions helps us choose the resize target.\n\nWe start by simply loading the CSV files and printing their shapes.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Load all three CSV files\ntrain_df     = pd.read_csv(DATA_DIR / 'train.csv')\nadd_train_df = pd.read_csv(DATA_DIR / 'additional_train.csv')\ntest_df      = pd.read_csv(DATA_DIR / 'test.csv')\n\nprint('train.csv          shape:', train_df.shape)\nprint('additional_train   shape:', add_train_df.shape)\nprint('test.csv           shape:', test_df.shape)\nprint()\nprint('--- First 5 rows of train.csv ---')\ndisplay(train_df.head())\nprint('--- First 5 rows of additional_train.csv ---')\ndisplay(add_train_df.head())","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:15:35.961449Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:15:35.961807Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:15:36.054079Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:15:35.961780Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:15:36.053233Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Chart 1 - Class Distribution\n\nA class distribution chart shows how many images exist for each pen. The ideal situation is a perfectly balanced bar chart where every pen has the same number of images. In reality, imbalance is common.\n\n**Why imbalance matters:** If pen 1 appears 5000 times and pen 8 appears only 200 times, the model can score 96% accuracy just by always predicting pen 1 - without ever learning anything about the other pens. Knowing this lets us decide whether to use class weights or oversampling to fix the problem.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Side-by-side class distribution for both CSVs\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 4))\npalette   = plt.cm.tab10.colors[:8]\n\nfor ax, df, title in [\n    (axes[0], train_df,     'train.csv'),\n    (axes[1], add_train_df, 'additional_train.csv'),\n]:\n    counts = df['pen_id'].value_counts().sort_index()\n    bars   = ax.bar(counts.index.astype(str), counts.values,\n                    color=palette, edgecolor='black', linewidth=0.8)\n    # Label each bar with its count\n    for bar, val in zip(bars, counts.values):\n        ax.text(bar.get_x() + bar.get_width() / 2,\n                bar.get_height() + counts.max() * 0.01,\n                str(val), ha='center', va='bottom', fontsize=9, fontweight='bold')\n    ax.set_title(f'Pen ID Distribution — {title}', fontsize=13, pad=10)\n    ax.set_xlabel('Pen ID', fontsize=11)\n    ax.set_ylabel('Number of images', fontsize=11)\n    ax.set_ylim(0, counts.max() * 1.15)\n    ax.spines[['top', 'right']].set_visible(False)\n    ratio = counts.max() / counts.min()\n    ax.annotate(f'Imbalance ratio: {ratio:.1f}x',\n                xy=(0.97, 0.95), xycoords='axes fraction',\n                ha='right', fontsize=9, color='crimson')\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig('eda_class_distribution.png', dpi=120, bbox_inches='tight')\nplt.show()\nprint('If the bars are roughly equal in height, the dataset is balanced.')\nprint('A large imbalance ratio (> 3x) may require class weighting.')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:15:36.055136Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:15:36.055389Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:15:36.751273Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:15:36.055367Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:15:36.750366Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Chart 2 - Sample Images per Pen Class\n\nNumbers tell us counts; images tell us what the model actually has to learn. We show 4 sample circles for each of the 8 pens arranged in a grid. Look closely at each row and ask yourself: can you visually tell the pens apart? Differences to look for include line thickness, ink darkness, smoothness of the stroke edge, and whether the ink bleeds into the paper.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# 8 x 4 sample image grid (one row per pen, four images per row)\nCOLS_PER_PEN = 4\npen_classes  = sorted(train_df['pen_id'].unique())\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(\n    len(pen_classes), COLS_PER_PEN,\n    figsize=(COLS_PER_PEN * 2.8, len(pen_classes) * 2.8)\n)\n\nfor row_idx, pen in enumerate(pen_classes):\n    pen_rows = train_df[train_df['pen_id'] == pen]\n    samples  = pen_rows.sample(\n        min(COLS_PER_PEN, len(pen_rows)), random_state=SEED\n    )\n    for col_idx in range(COLS_PER_PEN):\n        ax = axes[row_idx, col_idx]\n        ax.axis('off')\n        if col_idx < len(samples):\n            img_path = DATA_DIR / samples.iloc[col_idx]['image_path']\n            if img_path.exists():\n                img = Image.open(img_path).convert('RGB')\n                ax.imshow(img)\n            else:\n                ax.text(0.5, 0.5, 'File\\nmissing', ha='center',\n                        va='center', transform=ax.transAxes, color='red')\n        # Label the leftmost column with the pen name\n        if col_idx == 0:\n            ax.set_title(f'Pen {pen}', fontsize=10, loc='left',\n                         fontweight='bold', color=palette[pen - 1])\n\nfig.suptitle('Sample Circle Images — One Row per Pen', fontsize=15, y=1.01)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig('eda_sample_grid.png', dpi=80, bbox_inches='tight')\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:15:36.752385Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:15:36.752649Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:15:39.001806Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:15:36.752625Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:15:39.000744Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Chart 3 - Image Dimension Statistics\n\nNeural networks need a fixed input size. Before choosing our resize target (224×224) we check the actual dimensions of the scanned images. If all images are already close to 224×224 we lose very little information when resizing. If they are very large (e.g. 1000×1000) we might consider using a bigger resize target like 384×384 to preserve fine stroke details.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Sample 300 images and measure their width and height\nN_SAMPLE = 300\nsample_rows = train_df.sample(min(N_SAMPLE, len(train_df)), random_state=SEED)\n\nwidths, heights = [], []\nfor _, row in sample_rows.iterrows():\n    p = DATA_DIR / row['image_path']\n    if p.exists():\n        with Image.open(p) as img:\n            w, h = img.size\n            widths.append(w)\n            heights.append(h)\n\n# Print summary statistics\nprint(f'Sampled {len(widths)} images')\nprint(f'Width  — min: {min(widths)}   max: {max(widths)}   mean: {np.mean(widths):.0f}   std: {np.std(widths):.0f}')\nprint(f'Height — min: {min(heights)}   max: {max(heights)}   mean: {np.mean(heights):.0f}   std: {np.std(heights):.0f}')\n\n# Plot histograms of width and height\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(12, 3.5))\n\naxes[0].hist(widths,  bins=30, color='steelblue', edgecolor='white', alpha=0.85)\naxes[0].axvline(IMG_SIZE, color='red', linestyle='--', linewidth=1.5,\n                label=f'Resize target = {IMG_SIZE}')\naxes[0].set_title('Image Width Distribution', fontsize=12)\naxes[0].set_xlabel('Pixels'); axes[0].set_ylabel('Count')\naxes[0].legend(); axes[0].spines[['top', 'right']].set_visible(False)\n\naxes[1].hist(heights, bins=30, color='darkorange', edgecolor='white', alpha=0.85)\naxes[1].axvline(IMG_SIZE, color='red', linestyle='--', linewidth=1.5,\n                label=f'Resize target = {IMG_SIZE}')\naxes[1].set_title('Image Height Distribution', fontsize=12)\naxes[1].set_xlabel('Pixels'); axes[1].set_ylabel('Count')\naxes[1].legend(); axes[1].spines[['top', 'right']].set_visible(False)\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig('eda_dimensions.png', dpi=120, bbox_inches='tight')\nplt.show()\nprint('The red dashed line shows where we resize to. Most images should be near or larger than this line.')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:15:39.003255Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:15:39.003617Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:15:40.565827Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:15:39.003583Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:15:40.564691Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 3 - Data Loading\n\n### Step 1 - Merging the Training Data\n\nWe have two labelled files: `train.csv` and `additional_train.csv`. The additional file contains some rows where `writer_id == -1`, meaning we know the pen used but not which person drew it.\n\nThe toggle `INCLUDE_UNKNOWN_WRITERS` (set to `True` below) controls whether we include those rows. The pen label is still valid even when the writer is unknown, so including them generally helps. But if you suspect those images are lower quality, set it to `False` to exclude them.\n\n### Step 2 - Stratified Train / Validation Split\n\nWe split the merged data 80% for training and 20% for validation. The word 'stratified' means we make sure both halves contain the same proportion of each pen class. Without stratification, a random split might put almost all pen-5 images in training and leave none for validation — making it impossible to measure how well the model handles pen 5.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Merge training sources\nINCLUDE_UNKNOWN_WRITERS = True  # Set False to exclude writer_id == -1 rows\n\ncombined_df = pd.concat([train_df, add_train_df], ignore_index=True)\n\nif not INCLUDE_UNKNOWN_WRITERS:\n    before = len(combined_df)\n    # writer_id can be the integer -1 or the string '-1' depending on the file\n    combined_df = combined_df[\n        (combined_df['writer_id'] != -1) &\n        (combined_df['writer_id'] != '-1')\n    ].copy()\n    print(f'Excluded unknown writers: removed {before - len(combined_df)} rows')\n\n# Convert pen_id (1-8) to a 0-indexed label (0-7) that PyTorch loss expects\ncombined_df['label'] = combined_df['pen_id'].astype(int) - 1\n\nprint(f'Total labelled images: {len(combined_df):,}')\nprint('\\nPen distribution in combined set:')\nprint(combined_df['pen_id'].value_counts().sort_index().to_string())\n\n# Stratified 80 / 20 split\ntrain_split, val_split = train_test_split(\n    combined_df,\n    test_size=0.20,        # 20% of data goes to validation\n    random_state=SEED,\n    stratify=combined_df['label'],  # Preserve class proportions\n)\ntrain_split = train_split.reset_index(drop=True)\nval_split   = val_split.reset_index(drop=True)\n\nprint(f'\\nTrain split: {len(train_split):,} images')\nprint(f'Val   split: {len(val_split):,} images')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:15:40.567236Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:15:40.567576Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:15:40.612334Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:15:40.567543Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:15:40.611109Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Step 3 - Image Augmentation\n\nAugmentation is one of the most important tricks in computer vision. The idea is simple: instead of showing the model the exact same training images every epoch, we randomly modify each image slightly before feeding it in. This forces the model to learn the underlying pen stroke rather than memorising pixel positions.\n\n**Training augmentations explained one by one:**\n\n| Transform | What it does | Why it helps for circles |\n|-----------|-------------|-------------------------|\n| RandomResizedCrop | Crops a random part of the image and resizes to 224×224 | Teaches the model that the pen feature appears everywhere in the circle, not just the centre |\n| HorizontalFlip | Mirrors the image left-right | A circle is symmetric; the pen texture is the same from either side |\n| VerticalFlip | Mirrors top-bottom | Same reasoning as horizontal flip |\n| Rotate | Rotates by a random angle up to 30° | A circle looks the same from any angle |\n| ColorJitter | Randomly changes brightness, contrast, saturation | Simulates different scanner settings or lighting conditions |\n| ElasticTransform | Applies a rubber-sheet-like local warp | Simulates the way different writers press the pen at different angles |\n| CoarseDropout | Blacks out small random patches | Forces the model to use the whole stroke, not just one lucky patch |\n| CLAHE | Enhances local contrast | Brings out fine ink texture details that might be washed out in low-contrast scans |\n| Normalize | Subtracts ImageNet mean/std | Matches the pixel range the pretrained model was trained on |\n\nThe **validation transform** does nothing random only resize and normalize. This gives a fair, consistent measure of accuracy on untouched images.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# ImageNet normalisation constants\n# EfficientNetV2-S was pretrained on ImageNet with these exact mean and std values.\n# We must use the same values so the pixel distribution matches what the\n# model's pretrained weights expect.\nIMAGENET_MEAN = (0.485, 0.456, 0.406)\nIMAGENET_STD  = (0.229, 0.224, 0.225)\n\nprint(f'albumentations version: {A.__version__}')\n\n# Training augmentation pipeline (albumentations 2.x compatible)\ntrain_transform = A.Compose([\n\n    # FIXED: 2.x requires size=(H, W) tuple — 'height=' and 'width=' were removed.\n    # Randomly crops between 75-100% of the image then resizes to 224×224.\n    A.RandomResizedCrop(size=(IMG_SIZE, IMG_SIZE),\n                        scale=(0.75, 1.0), ratio=(0.9, 1.1), p=1.0),\n\n    # Orientation flips — free augmentation since circles are symmetric\n    A.HorizontalFlip(p=0.5),\n    A.VerticalFlip(p=0.3),\n\n    # border_mode still accepted in 2.x, but we drop it to keep code clean\n    A.Rotate(limit=30, p=0.6),\n\n    # Photometric variation — simulates different scanner settings\n    A.ColorJitter(brightness=0.3, contrast=0.3,\n                  saturation=0.2, hue=0.05, p=0.7),\n\n    # FIXED: alpha_affine removed in 2.x — just use alpha and sigma\n    A.ElasticTransform(alpha=120, sigma=6, p=0.25),\n\n    # FIXED: old max_holes / max_height / fill_value params renamed in 2.x.\n    # num_holes_range=(min, max), hole_height_range=(min, max), fill= (not fill_value=)\n    A.CoarseDropout(\n        num_holes_range=(1, 8),\n        hole_height_range=(8, IMG_SIZE // 8),\n        hole_width_range=(8, IMG_SIZE // 8),\n        fill=0,                              # ← was: fill_value=0\n        p=0.3,\n    ),\n\n    # Enhance local contrast to reveal fine ink texture\n    A.CLAHE(clip_limit=4.0, tile_grid_size=(8, 8), p=0.25),\n\n    # Match ImageNet normalisation and convert to (C, H, W) tensor\n    A.Normalize(mean=IMAGENET_MEAN, std=IMAGENET_STD),\n    ToTensorV2(),\n])\n\n# Validation / test pipeline (no randomness)\n# Resize still uses height= / width= in 2.x — no change needed here.\nval_transform = A.Compose([\n    A.Resize(height=IMG_SIZE, width=IMG_SIZE),\n    A.Normalize(mean=IMAGENET_MEAN, std=IMAGENET_STD),\n    ToTensorV2(),\n])\n\nprint(f'Training pipeline   : {len(train_transform.transforms)} transforms')\nprint(f'Validation pipeline : {len(val_transform.transforms)} transforms (resize + normalise only)')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:15:40.613634Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:15:40.614053Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:15:40.634559Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:15:40.614017Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:15:40.633368Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Step 4 - PyTorch Dataset and DataLoader\n\nA **Dataset** is a Python class that knows how to retrieve one training example. Think of it as a smart list: you ask for item number 500, it opens the 500th image file, applies the augmentation pipeline, and hands you back a (tensor, label) pair.\n\nA **DataLoader** wraps the Dataset and handles the mechanics of batching. It uses background worker processes (`num_workers=2`) to pre-load the next batch while the GPU is processing the current one, keeping the GPU busy at all times. `pin_memory=True` speeds up the transfer of image data from CPU RAM to GPU memory.\n\nThe `_load_image` method includes a graceful fallback: if an image file is missing, it returns a neutral grey image instead of crashing. This makes the pipeline robust to small dataset issues.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"class CircleIDDataset(Dataset):\n    \"\"\"\n    Custom PyTorch Dataset for the CircleID pen classification task.\n\n    For a training row it returns (image_tensor, label).\n    For a test row (is_test=True) it returns (image_tensor, image_id).\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, df: pd.DataFrame, data_dir: Path,\n                 transform=None, is_test: bool = False):\n        self.df        = df.reset_index(drop=True)\n        self.data_dir  = data_dir\n        self.transform = transform\n        self.is_test   = is_test\n\n    def __len__(self) -> int:\n        return len(self.df)\n\n    def _load_image(self, path: Path) -> np.ndarray:\n        '''Load a single PNG as an RGB numpy array.'''\n        if path.exists():\n            return np.array(Image.open(path).convert('RGB'))\n        # If the file is missing, return a neutral grey image instead of crashing\n        return np.full((IMG_SIZE, IMG_SIZE, 3), 128, dtype=np.uint8)\n\n    def __getitem__(self, idx: int):\n        row   = self.df.iloc[idx]\n        image = self._load_image(self.data_dir / row['image_path'])\n\n        if self.transform is not None:\n            image = self.transform(image=image)['image']  # returns (C, H, W) tensor\n        else:\n            image = torch.from_numpy(image.transpose(2, 0, 1)).float() / 255.0\n\n        if self.is_test:\n            return image, str(row['image_id'])\n        return image, int(row['label'])\n\n\n# Build Dataset objects \ntrain_dataset = CircleIDDataset(train_split, DATA_DIR, transform=train_transform)\nval_dataset   = CircleIDDataset(val_split,   DATA_DIR, transform=val_transform)\ntest_dataset  = CircleIDDataset(test_df,     DATA_DIR, transform=val_transform,\n                                is_test=True)\n\n# Build DataLoader objects\n# drop_last=True in the train loader drops the final incomplete batch so that\n# batch normalisation layers always receive a full batch of 32 images.\ntrain_loader = DataLoader(train_dataset, batch_size=BATCH_SIZE, shuffle=True,\n                          num_workers=NUM_WORKERS, pin_memory=True, drop_last=True)\nval_loader   = DataLoader(val_dataset,   batch_size=BATCH_SIZE, shuffle=False,\n                          num_workers=NUM_WORKERS, pin_memory=True)\ntest_loader  = DataLoader(test_dataset,  batch_size=BATCH_SIZE, shuffle=False,\n                          num_workers=NUM_WORKERS, pin_memory=True)\n\n# Quick sanity check\nsample_imgs, sample_labels = next(iter(train_loader))\nprint(f'Train batches : {len(train_loader)}')\nprint(f'Val   batches : {len(val_loader)}')\nprint(f'Test  batches : {len(test_loader)}')\nprint(f'One batch shape : {sample_imgs.shape}  (batch_size x channels x H x W)')\nprint(f'Label range     : {sample_labels.min().item()} to {sample_labels.max().item()}')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:15:40.637732Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:15:40.638176Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:15:41.931681Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:15:40.638147Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:15:41.930629Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 4 - Model Architecture\n\n### Transfer Learning: Standing on the Shoulders of Giants\n\nTraining a deep neural network from scratch requires millions of images and weeks of GPU time. Transfer learning avoids this by reusing a network that was already trained on a massive dataset.\n\nWe use **EfficientNetV2-S**, which was trained on ImageNet (1.2 million photos, 1000 categories). During that training it learned to detect edges, textures, colour gradients, and shapes. We discard only the final 1000-class prediction layer and replace it with a new layer that predicts 8 pen classes. The rest of the network keeps its pretrained weights and will be fine-tuned on our circle images.\n\n**Our custom head:**\n\n```\nEfficientNetV2-S backbone  →  feature vector (e.g. 1280 numbers)\n     LayerNorm             →  stabilises the scale of the features\n     Dropout(0.3)          →  randomly zeros 30% of features to prevent overfitting\n     Linear(feat_dim, 8)   →  maps features to 8 pen-class scores\n```\n\n**LayerNorm** normalises the feature vector so all values have a consistent scale, which helps the dropout and linear layers work better.\n\n**Dropout(0.3)** randomly turns off 30% of neurons during each training step. This forces the remaining neurons to do the work, preventing any single neuron from becoming too dominant. During validation, dropout is turned off automatically.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"MODEL_NAME   = 'tf_efficientnetv2_s.in21k_ft_in1k'\n\nprint(f'Device     : {DEVICE}')\nprint(f'timm       : {timm.__version__}')\nprint(f'Model      : {MODEL_NAME}')\n\n# ── Model definition ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\ndef build_model(model_name=MODEL_NAME, num_classes=NUM_CLASSES, pretrained=True):\n    '''\n    Loads a pretrained EfficientNetV2-S backbone and attaches a custom head:\n        LayerNorm → Dropout(0.3) → Linear(feat_dim, 8)\n    '''\n    backbone = timm.create_model(model_name, pretrained=pretrained, num_classes=0)\n\n    # Measure the feature vector size the backbone produces\n    with torch.no_grad():\n        feat_dim = backbone(torch.zeros(1, 3, IMG_SIZE, IMG_SIZE)).shape[-1]\n\n    # Replace the head with our 8-class classifier\n    backbone.head = nn.Sequential(\n        nn.LayerNorm(feat_dim),\n        nn.Dropout(p=0.3),\n        nn.Linear(feat_dim, num_classes),\n    )\n    return backbone, feat_dim\n\n\nmodel, feat_dim = build_model()\nmodel = model.to(DEVICE)\n\ntotal_params     = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters())\ntrainable_params = sum(p.numel() for p in model.parameters() if p.requires_grad)\nprint(f'\\nBackbone feature dim : {feat_dim}')\nprint(f'Total params         : {total_params:,}')\nprint(f'Trainable params     : {trainable_params:,}')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:15:41.933362Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:15:41.933741Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:15:42.784142Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:15:41.933694Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:15:42.782801Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Loss Function, Optimiser, and Learning Rate Scheduler\n\n**Label-smoothed CrossEntropyLoss** is the standard loss for multi-class classification. Normally the loss trains the model to predict exactly 1.0 for the correct class and 0.0 for all others. Label smoothing of 0.1 softens this to 0.9 / 0.1-spread, which prevents the model from becoming overconfident and helps it generalise to unseen writers.\n\n**AdamW optimiser** updates the weights based on the gradient of the loss. The 'W' means 'weight decay' — a penalty that discourages very large weights, similar to how a teacher penalises a student who memorises rather than understands. We use different weight decay settings for the main weights vs. bias and normalisation parameters (which should not be penalised).\n\n**Warm-up + cosine decay scheduler** controls how big the weight-update steps are over time. For the first 5% of training we gradually increase the learning rate from near-zero to the full value (warm-up). Then we slowly decrease it following a cosine curve until it reaches near-zero at the end of training. Large steps early, tiny precise steps later.\n\n**GradScaler (mixed precision)** lets most calculations use 16-bit floating point instead of 32-bit. This roughly doubles speed on modern GPUs with no meaningful accuracy loss. The scaler prevents gradients from underflowing to zero in 16-bit.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Loss function\ncriterion = nn.CrossEntropyLoss(label_smoothing=LABEL_SMOOTH)\n\n# Optimiser with per-group weight decay\n# Bias and normalisation weights are excluded from weight decay because\n# penalising them hurts rather than helps generalisation.\nNO_DECAY = {'bias', 'LayerNorm.weight', 'layer_norm.weight', 'norm'}\nparam_groups = [\n    {\n        'params': [p for n, p in model.named_parameters()\n                   if not any(nd in n for nd in NO_DECAY)],\n        'weight_decay': WEIGHT_DECAY,\n    },\n    {\n        'params': [p for n, p in model.named_parameters()\n                   if any(nd in n for nd in NO_DECAY)],\n        'weight_decay': 0.0,\n    },\n]\noptimizer = optim.AdamW(param_groups, lr=LR)\n\n# Warm-up + cosine decay scheduler\ntotal_steps  = NUM_EPOCHS * len(train_loader)\nwarmup_steps = int(0.05 * total_steps)   # First 5% of steps = warm-up phase\n\ndef lr_lambda(step: int) -> float:\n    '''Linear ramp-up for warmup_steps, then smooth cosine decay to ~0.'''\n    if step < warmup_steps:\n        return float(step) / float(max(1, warmup_steps))\n    progress = float(step - warmup_steps) / float(max(1, total_steps - warmup_steps))\n    return max(0.0, 0.5 * (1.0 + np.cos(np.pi * progress)))\n\nscheduler = optim.lr_scheduler.LambdaLR(optimizer, lr_lambda)\n\n# Mixed precision scaler\n# Enabled only on GPU — on CPU it adds overhead without any speed benefit.\nscaler = GradScaler(enabled=(DEVICE.type == 'cuda'))\n\nprint(f'\\nLoss        : CrossEntropyLoss  label_smoothing={LABEL_SMOOTH}')\nprint(f'Optimiser   : AdamW  |  LR={LR}  |  weight_decay={WEIGHT_DECAY}')\nprint(f'Total steps : {total_steps:,}  (warm-up: {warmup_steps} steps)')\nprint(f'Mixed prec. : {DEVICE.type == \"cuda\"}')\nprint('\\nAll components ready — proceed to the training loop.')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:15:42.785480Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:15:42.785821Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:15:42.804156Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:15:42.785790Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:15:42.803151Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 5 - Training Loop\n\nOne training **epoch** means the model has seen every image in the training set once. We run 20 epochs total, so the model sees every image 20 times (each time with a different random augmentation).\n\n**What happens in each training step:**\n\n1. Load a batch of 32 images and their labels\n2. Run them through the model to get 8-class predictions (forward pass)\n3. Compute the loss: how wrong were those predictions?\n4. Run backpropagation: compute how much each weight contributed to the error\n5. Gradient clipping: cap very large gradients at 1.0 so no single step is too big\n6. Optimiser step: nudge every weight slightly in the direction that reduces the loss\n7. Scheduler step: update the learning rate\n\nAfter each epoch we run the model on the **validation set** without updating any weights. This gives an honest estimate of how well the model generalises. We save the model whenever the validation accuracy improves — that saved checkpoint is our best model.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def train_one_epoch(model, loader, criterion, optimizer,\n                    scheduler, scaler, device, epoch: int) -> float:\n    '''\n    Runs one full pass over the training data.\n    Returns the average loss across all batches.\n    '''\n    model.train()  # Enables dropout and batch-norm training behaviour\n    total_loss = 0.0\n    pbar = tqdm(loader, desc=f'[Epoch {epoch:02d} Train]', leave=False)\n\n    for imgs, labels in pbar:\n        imgs   = imgs.to(device, non_blocking=True)\n        labels = labels.to(device, non_blocking=True)\n\n        optimizer.zero_grad()  # Clear gradients from the previous step\n\n        # autocast switches to 16-bit arithmetic on GPU for speed\n        with autocast(enabled=(device.type == 'cuda')):\n            logits = model(imgs)           # Shape: (32, 8)\n            loss   = criterion(logits, labels)\n\n        scaler.scale(loss).backward()      # Backprop with scaled gradients\n\n        # Unscale then clip: prevents any single gradient from being too large\n        scaler.unscale_(optimizer)\n        nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(model.parameters(), max_norm=1.0)\n\n        scaler.step(optimizer)             # Update model weights\n        scaler.update()                    # Update the scaler for next step\n        scheduler.step()                   # Update learning rate\n\n        total_loss += loss.item() * imgs.size(0)\n        pbar.set_postfix(\n            loss=f'{loss.item():.4f}',\n            lr=f'{scheduler.get_last_lr()[0]:.2e}'\n        )\n\n    return total_loss / len(loader.dataset)\n\n\n@torch.no_grad()  # Disable gradient computation — saves memory and speeds up val\ndef validate(model, loader, criterion, device) -> tuple:\n    '''\n    Evaluates the model on the validation set.\n    Returns (average_loss, accuracy_fraction).\n    '''\n    model.eval()   # Disables dropout so predictions are deterministic\n    total_loss, n_correct = 0.0, 0\n\n    for imgs, labels in tqdm(loader, desc='[Val]', leave=False):\n        imgs   = imgs.to(device, non_blocking=True)\n        labels = labels.to(device, non_blocking=True)\n\n        with autocast(enabled=(device.type == 'cuda')):\n            logits = model(imgs)\n            loss   = criterion(logits, labels)\n\n        preds      = logits.argmax(dim=1)   # Predicted class = highest score\n        n_correct  += (preds == labels).sum().item()\n        total_loss += loss.item() * imgs.size(0)\n\n    avg_loss = total_loss / len(loader.dataset)\n    accuracy = n_correct  / len(loader.dataset)\n    return avg_loss, accuracy\n\nprint('Training and validation functions defined.')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:15:42.805434Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:15:42.805806Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:15:42.828098Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:15:42.805761Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:15:42.826949Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Now we run the main training loop for 20 epochs. Watch the `val_acc` column - it should rise over time. The marker `<-- best` appears whenever the model beats its previous best validation accuracy and a checkpoint is saved to disk.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Skip training if a saved checkpoint already exists\n# This means you can re-run the whole notebook without waiting 4 hours again.\n# To force a fresh training run, delete best_model.pth and run this cell again.\n\nif CHECKPOINT.exists():\n    print(f'Checkpoint found at {CHECKPOINT} — loading saved results, skipping training.')\n    _ckpt = torch.load(CHECKPOINT, map_location=DEVICE)\n    model.load_state_dict(_ckpt['model_state'])\n    best_val_acc = _ckpt['val_acc']\n    best_epoch   = _ckpt['epoch']\n    # Rebuild history as a single-entry record so later cells don't break\n    history = {\n        'train_loss': [_ckpt.get('train_loss', 0.0)],\n        'val_loss'  : [_ckpt['val_loss']],\n        'val_acc'   : [_ckpt['val_acc']],\n    }\n    print(f'Loaded  : epoch {best_epoch + 1}  val_acc={best_val_acc:.4f}')\n    print('To retrain: delete best_model.pth and re-run this cell.')\n\nelse:\n    # Main training loop\n    history = {'train_loss': [], 'val_loss': [], 'val_acc': []}\n    best_val_acc = 0.0\n    best_epoch   = 0\n\n    print(f'Training {MODEL_NAME} for {NUM_EPOCHS} epochs on {DEVICE}')\n    print('=' * 70)\n\n    for epoch in range(NUM_EPOCHS):\n        train_loss        = train_one_epoch(\n            model, train_loader, criterion,\n            optimizer, scheduler, scaler, DEVICE, epoch + 1\n        )\n        val_loss, val_acc = validate(model, val_loader, criterion, DEVICE)\n\n        history['train_loss'].append(train_loss)\n        history['val_loss'].append(val_loss)\n        history['val_acc'].append(val_acc)\n\n        marker = ''\n        if val_acc > best_val_acc:\n            best_val_acc = val_acc\n            best_epoch   = epoch\n            torch.save({\n                'epoch'      : epoch,\n                'model_state': model.state_dict(),\n                'val_acc'    : val_acc,\n                'val_loss'   : val_loss,\n                'train_loss' : train_loss,   # also save train_loss for history reload\n            }, CHECKPOINT)\n            marker = '  <-- best'\n\n        current_lr = scheduler.get_last_lr()[0]\n        print(\n            f'Epoch {epoch+1:02d}/{NUM_EPOCHS}  '\n            f'train_loss={train_loss:.4f}  '\n            f'val_loss={val_loss:.4f}  '\n            f'val_acc={val_acc:.4f}  '\n            f'lr={current_lr:.2e}'\n            f'{marker}'\n        )\n\n    print('=' * 70)\n    print(f'Best validation accuracy: {best_val_acc:.4f} at epoch {best_epoch + 1}')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:15:42.829646Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:15:42.830122Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:15:43.036178Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:15:42.830087Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:15:43.035221Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Training Curves\n\nPlotting the loss and accuracy over epochs is the best way to diagnose training problems:\n\n- **Both losses decreasing** - training is working normally.\n- **Train loss low but val loss rising** - overfitting. The model is memorising training images. Try more augmentation or reducing learning rate.\n- **Both losses plateau early** - underfitting. Try a higher learning rate or more epochs.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Plot training curves\nepochs_x = range(1, len(history['train_loss']) + 1)\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 4))\n\n# Loss curve\naxes[0].plot(epochs_x, history['train_loss'], marker='o', ms=4, label='Train Loss')\naxes[0].plot(epochs_x, history['val_loss'],   marker='s', ms=4, label='Val Loss')\naxes[0].axvline(best_epoch + 1, color='red', linestyle='--', alpha=0.7,\n                label=f'Best epoch ({best_epoch+1})')\naxes[0].set_title('Loss vs. Epoch', fontsize=13)\naxes[0].set_xlabel('Epoch'); axes[0].set_ylabel('Loss')\naxes[0].legend(); axes[0].grid(alpha=0.3)\naxes[0].spines[['top', 'right']].set_visible(False)\n\n# Accuracy curve\naxes[1].plot(epochs_x, [a * 100 for a in history['val_acc']],\n             color='forestgreen', marker='D', ms=4, label='Val Accuracy')\naxes[1].axvline(best_epoch + 1, color='red', linestyle='--', alpha=0.7,\n                label=f'Best epoch ({best_epoch+1})')\naxes[1].set_title('Validation Accuracy vs. Epoch', fontsize=13)\naxes[1].set_xlabel('Epoch'); axes[1].set_ylabel('Accuracy (%)')\naxes[1].legend(); axes[1].grid(alpha=0.3)\naxes[1].spines[['top', 'right']].set_visible(False)\n\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig('training_curves.png', dpi=120, bbox_inches='tight')\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:15:43.037458Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:15:43.037843Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:15:43.780095Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:15:43.037792Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:15:43.778882Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 6 - Evaluation\n\nTraining is done. Now we load the **best checkpoint** (the epoch with the highest validation accuracy that was saved earlier) and do a thorough evaluation.\n\n**Metrics we compute:**\n\n- **Top-1 Accuracy** - the fraction of images where the model's top prediction is correct. This is the competition's primary metric.\n- **Macro F1** - computes the F1 score for each pen class separately and averages them. This penalises models that do well on common pens but fail on rare ones.\n\n**Confusion matrix** - a grid where row = true pen, column = predicted pen. A perfect model fills only the main diagonal. Off-diagonal numbers reveal which pen pairs the model most often confuses.\n\n**Misclassified examples** - we show actual images the model got wrong, together with what it predicted and how confident it was. This often reveals whether the model is making sensible mistakes (confusing two visually similar pens) or nonsensical ones.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Load the best checkpoint\ncheckpoint = torch.load(CHECKPOINT, map_location=DEVICE)\nmodel.load_state_dict(checkpoint['model_state'])\nmodel.eval()\nprint(f\"Loaded checkpoint from epoch {checkpoint['epoch']+1}  \"\n      f\"(saved val_acc = {checkpoint['val_acc']:.4f})\")\n\n# Collect all predictions on the validation set\nall_preds, all_labels, all_probs = [], [], []\n\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    for imgs, labels in tqdm(val_loader, desc='Evaluating'):\n        imgs = imgs.to(DEVICE, non_blocking=True)\n        with autocast(enabled=(DEVICE.type == 'cuda')):\n            logits = model(imgs)\n        probs = torch.softmax(logits, dim=1).cpu().numpy()  # Convert to probabilities\n        preds = probs.argmax(axis=1)                        # Predicted class\n        all_probs.extend(probs)\n        all_preds.extend(preds)\n        all_labels.extend(labels.numpy())\n\nall_preds  = np.array(all_preds)\nall_labels = np.array(all_labels)\nall_probs  = np.array(all_probs)\n\n# Overall metrics\naccuracy = (all_preds == all_labels).mean()\nmacro_f1 = f1_score(all_labels, all_preds, average='macro')\n\nprint(f'\\n{\"─\"*45}')\nprint(f'  Validation Accuracy : {accuracy:.4f}  ({accuracy*100:.2f}%)')\nprint(f'  Macro F1 Score      : {macro_f1:.4f}')\nprint(f'{\"─\"*45}')\n\n# Per-class accuracy table\nclass_names = [f'pen_{i+1}' for i in range(NUM_CLASSES)]\nprint('\\nPer-class accuracy:')\nfor c in range(NUM_CLASSES):\n    mask     = all_labels == c\n    cls_acc  = (all_preds[mask] == c).mean() if mask.sum() > 0 else 0.0\n    bar_fill = int(cls_acc * 30)\n    print(f'  {class_names[c]}: {cls_acc:.4f}  [{\"#\"*bar_fill}{\".\"*(30-bar_fill)}]')","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:18:40.930689Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:18:40.931275Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:29:51.899386Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:18:40.931234Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:29:51.898129Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The confusion matrix below shows where the model makes mistakes. Each cell (row r, column c) contains the number of images from true pen r that the model predicted as pen c. A large number outside the diagonal means those two pens are frequently confused with each other.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Confusion matrix\ncm = confusion_matrix(all_labels, all_preds)\n\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(9, 7))\ndisp = ConfusionMatrixDisplay(confusion_matrix=cm, display_labels=class_names)\ndisp.plot(ax=ax, colorbar=True, cmap='Blues', values_format='d')\nax.set_title(\n    f'Confusion Matrix  |  Acc={accuracy:.4f}  Macro-F1={macro_f1:.4f}',\n    fontsize=12, pad=12\n)\nax.set_xlabel('Predicted Pen', fontsize=11)\nax.set_ylabel('True Pen', fontsize=11)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig('confusion_matrix.png', dpi=120, bbox_inches='tight')\nplt.show()\n\n# Most confused pairs\nprint('Top 5 most confused pen pairs (excluding diagonal):')\ncm_off = cm.copy()\nnp.fill_diagonal(cm_off, 0)\nfor rank in range(5):\n    r, c = np.unravel_index(cm_off.argmax(), cm_off.shape)\n    print(f'  {rank+1}. True=pen_{r+1}  Predicted=pen_{c+1}  Count={cm_off[r,c]}')\n    cm_off[r, c] = 0","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:30:13.565732Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:30:13.566946Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:30:24.923379Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:30:13.566856Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:30:24.922291Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Looking at actual misclassified images helps us understand whether the model's errors are reasonable. Each image below shows the true pen label, the model's wrong prediction, and the confidence score (how sure the model was — higher is worse for wrong predictions).","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# Visualise misclassified examples\nmisclassified_idx = np.where(all_preds != all_labels)[0]\nprint(f'Misclassified: {len(misclassified_idx)} / {len(all_labels)} '\n      f'({len(misclassified_idx)/len(all_labels)*100:.1f}%)')\n\nN_SHOW = min(16, len(misclassified_idx))\nchosen = np.random.choice(misclassified_idx, N_SHOW, replace=False)\nCOLS   = 4\nROWS   = (N_SHOW + COLS - 1) // COLS\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(ROWS, COLS, figsize=(COLS * 3.2, ROWS * 3.2))\naxes      = axes.flatten()\n\nfor plot_i, val_i in enumerate(chosen):\n    row  = val_split.iloc[val_i]\n    path = DATA_DIR / row['image_path']\n    ax   = axes[plot_i]\n\n    if path.exists():\n        ax.imshow(Image.open(path).convert('RGB'))\n    else:\n        ax.set_facecolor('#ddd')\n\n    true_pen = class_names[all_labels[val_i]]\n    pred_pen = class_names[all_preds[val_i]]\n    conf     = all_probs[val_i, all_preds[val_i]] * 100\n    ax.set_title(f'True: {true_pen}\\nPred: {pred_pen} ({conf:.0f}% conf)',\n                 fontsize=8.5, color='crimson')\n    ax.axis('off')\n\nfor ax in axes[N_SHOW:]:\n    ax.axis('off')\n\nfig.suptitle('Misclassified Images — True Label vs. Model Prediction',\n             fontsize=13, y=1.01)\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.savefig('misclassified.png', dpi=80, bbox_inches='tight')\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-03-12T01:31:01.870581Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-03-12T01:31:01.871122Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-03-12T01:31:04.154007Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-03-12T01:31:01.871089Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-03-12T01:31:04.153073Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":null},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"---\n## 7 - Test-Time Augmentation (TTA) and Submission\n\n### What is Test-Time Augmentation?\n\nNormally during inference we feed one clean version of each test image into the model and take whatever prediction comes out. TTA does something smarter: for each test image we create **5 different versions** of it (one centre-crop and four randomly augmented crops and flips), run all 5 through the model, and **average the probability scores** before making the final decision.\n\nThink of it like asking 5 different experts to look at the same circle drawing (each from a slightly different angle or zoom level) and then taking the majority vote. The average is more reliable than any single view alone, especially for borderline cases where the ink texture might be slightly obscured in one crop but visible in another.\n\n**Why it works:** A circle drawn with pen 3 is still drawn with pen 3 when you flip it or look at a different part of it. The pen's ink texture appears everywhere in the circle. Averaging over 5 views reduces the noise in any single prediction.\n\nTypical accuracy gain from TTA: **+0.3% to +1.0%**, with no extra training needed.","metadata":{}}]}